Intrauterine Device (IUD) Removal
General Practice Other
Speculum visualization of strings, Gentle outward traction, Immediate intact extraction, Mild transient cramping

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-022
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe lower pelvic pain and suspected partial expulsion of an intrauterine device, requiring immediate evaluation and extraction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a patient experiencing sudden, severe, cramping pelvic pain accompanied by the palpable sensation of a hard plastic object protruding through the cervical os into the vaginal canal. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to rule out uterine perforation, confirm the partial expulsion, and perform an immediate extraction of the displaced device to relieve the acute cervical distension and pain. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period would have subjected the patient to unmanageable pain and a high risk of ascending pelvic infection overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I got a sudden terrible cramp, and now I can physically feel the hard plastic of my IUD sitting low in my vagina, and it hurts constantly."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a request for removal of a previously inserted levonorgestrel-releasing intrauterine device due to an acute mechanical complication. The device was inserted two years ago. One hour ago, while lifting a heavy box, the patient experienced a sudden, severe spasm of lower abdominal pain, followed by the sensation of a foreign body in the vaginal vault. The patient is experiencing unacceptable side effects, specifically intense, spasmodic pelvic pain mimicking labor contractions, caused by the device physically wedged in the cervical canal. The patient denies heavy bleeding, fever, or foul-smelling discharge.
Functional Status: Guarded, lying strictly in the fetal position due to the severe, cramping cervical pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: High acute distress; patient lives alone.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 135/85 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.9 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 65 Kg
General: Visibly uncomfortable, wincing, diaphoretic.
Physical Exam: Abdominal: Soft, tender to deep palpation in the suprapubic region, no rebound or guarding. Pelvic: Visualization of the cervix utilizing a vaginal speculum confirms the stem of the IUD is actively protruding 2cm through the external os. There is no active hemorrhage or purulent discharge. The procedure mandates the application of gentle steady outward traction on the strings using ring forceps, resulting in the immediate intact extraction of the device. Expected investigation results include direct clinical visualization of the completely intact removed device confirming total extraction from the uterine cavity.
Environmental Observations: Clean, private bedroom allowing for safe speculum examination.
Medication Reconciliation: Ibuprofen 400mg taken 30 minutes ago with zero relief.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Intrauterine Device (IUD) Removal (Acute Partial Expulsion) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Direct visualization of the mechanical cause of the pain (the expelled IUD in the cervix) and a negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Afebrile status, absence of purulent cervical discharge, and the sudden, mechanical onset of the pain.

Uterine Perforation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The device is visibly expelling outward through the anatomical os rather than migrating into the peritoneal cavity, and the pain is resolving instantly upon extraction.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced an acute, painful partial expulsion of an IUD, where the rigid device became lodged in the cervical canal, causing severe, continuous cramping. Telephone triage correctly identified the mechanical nature of the emergency. Urgent face-to-face assessment was mandated to perform a speculum examination, definitively diagnose the partial expulsion, and immediately extract the device. The extraction provided instantaneous relief of the severe cervical distension pain. Deferring this simple but critical intervention until the next in-hours period would have guaranteed an unnecessary emergency department presentation for unmanageable pain overnight.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Performed a sterile speculum examination. Grasped the protruding IUD strings with ring forceps and applied gentle outward traction, successfully achieving an intact removal. The patient experienced mild transient lower abdominal cramping during the removal process, followed immediately by total relief of the severe presenting pain. Minimal vaginal spotting immediately post-procedure was noted.
Positive Behavioral Change: Because you are now immediately fertile, commit to placing a box of condoms directly on top of your pillow tonight. This unavoidable physical barrier acts as a mandatory visual prompt to use alternative contraception immediately, bridging the gap until you can consult your regular doctor for a new, long-term plan.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Speculum and ring forceps (utilized from clinical kit).
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days to discuss alternative contraceptive options.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or present to the nearest Emergency Department if you develop a sudden high fever, foul-smelling vaginal discharge, or if heavy bleeding (soaking more than one pad an hour) begins overnight.

